PLACES=[
N('kingston-mills-lockstation','Kingston Mills locks and blockhouse','Kingston','on','canal','standing',44.29328,-76.44117,'site','public','drive','canal','1784–',
  "The King's Mill, a grist mill and saw mill built by the British government at the Cataraqui Falls in 1784, was the first mill on what became the Rideau Canal, and the road cut to it from Cataraqui is said to be the first road in Upper Canada. From 1827 the falls were cleared for the southernmost lockstation of the canal. A blockhouse was begun here in 1832, one of only four completed on the Rideau, and soldiers were stationed in it again in the 1860s during the American Civil War. A Grand Trunk railway bridge crossed the lower locks from 1853, and a power station was built below the canal dam in 1913–14.",
  "A flight of three locks in a rock gorge with a fourth, detached upper lock and a turning basin. The CN main line crosses the lower flight on a swing bridge (the present one is of 1988). The blockhouse has a stone base about 7.3 m square under a squared-timber upper storey and has been restored to its 1830s look. The lockmaster's house of 1904, now called the Anglin Centre, and a stone arch dam with its waste weir. Interior of the blockhouse not checked.",
  'A Loyalist mill site turned into the first lock of the Rideau, with a train passing over the chamber.',
  RCHOURS+' The lockstation is at 563 Kingston Mills Road; Parks Canada lists parking, picnic tables and washrooms on site.',
  [W('Kingston Mills'),L('Parks Canada: Kingston Mills','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/visit/posteeclusage-lockstation/ecluse-lock-kingston-mills'),
   L('Parks Canada: History of the Kingston Mills lockstation','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/histoire-history/histoire-decluse-lock-history/kingstonmills'),RCH],
  th=[44.29208,-76.44421],thn='Kingston Mills Road parking',brick='?'),
N('jones-falls-stone-arch-dam','Jones Falls: the Great Stone Arch Dam and lockstation','Rideau Lakes','on','canal','standing',44.54873,-76.23779,'site','public','walk','canal','1827–1832',
  "John Redpath, of the contracting partners Thomas McKay and John Redpath, built this lockstation between 1827 and 1832 to climb a mile of rapids that fell about 18 m out of Sand Lake. The answer was a stone arch dam about 18 m high and 107 m long, which Parks Canada and rideau-info.com both call the highest dam in North America when it was finished in 1831. A guardhouse followed in 1838, after the Upper Canada Rebellion. The defensible lockmaster's house on the hill (1840 in Parks Canada's history, 1841 in the federal heritage record) is called Sweeney House after Peter Sweeney, lockmaster from 1839 to 1871, whose diary survives. A stone blacksmith's shop of 1843 made Jones Falls a repair depot for the canal's iron work.",
  "The arch dam, which rideau-info.com says stands for the most part exactly as built (the power station and weir beside it are later). A flight of three locks, a turning basin and an upper lock, the highest lift on the Rideau at about 18 m. Sweeney House, a one-storey hipped-roof house of rough-faced stone with a closed defensible porch, restored to its period in the early 1980s, and the cube-shaped stone blacksmith's shop with a log floor and the forge near the middle of the room. Rideau-info.com says Parks Canada stopped opening the house and the shop to the public in 2022; whether they open now is not checked.",
  'In 1831 the highest dam on the continent, built of hand-cut stone in the bush.',
  RCHOURS+' The lockstation is at 182 Lock Road, off Jones Falls Road (County Road 11), about 4 km west of Highway 15; Parks Canada lists parking, scenic trails and a visitor centre.',
  [L('Parks Canada: Jones Falls','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/visit/posteeclusage-lockstation/ecluse-lock-jones-falls'),
   L('Parks Canada: History of the Jones Falls lockstation','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/histoire-history/histoire-decluse-lock-history/jonesfalls'),
   L('rideau-info.com: Jones Falls locks 39–42','https://rideau-info.com/canal/locks/39-42-jonesfalls.html'),
   L("Federal heritage record: Blacksmith's Shop, Jones Falls",'https://www.pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_fhbro_eng.aspx?id=3888'),
   L("Federal heritage record: Defensible Lockmaster's House, Jones Falls",'https://pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_fhbro_eng.aspx?id=3889'),RCH],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('davis-lockmasters-house','Davis Lock defensible lockmaster\'s house','Rideau Lakes','on','canal','standing',44.56278,-76.29194,'site','public','drive','canal','1842–',
  "William Davis Jr. had a sawmill here from about 1818–1820, which Colonel By bought out and destroyed to build the canal. Parks Canada calls Davis a \"solitude lock\", one of the more remote on the Rideau. Its stone lockmaster's house was built in 1842 and housed the lockmaster until 1959; Parks Canada says it was fully restored in 1999, and rideau-info.com calls it the best preserved defensible lockmaster's house on the Rideau.",
  "The 1842 stone lockmaster's house beside a single lock, and a view into Sand Lake that rideau-info.com says is, buoys aside, what Colonel By would have seen in 1832. Parks Canada lists \"historic accommodation\" at Davis; whether the house is the rental and whether visitors can go in is not checked.",
  'The quietest lock on the canal, with its 1842 stone house.',
  RCHOURS+' The lockstation is at 1822 Davis Lock Road, near Elgin; Parks Canada lists parking. The nearest lot OpenStreetMap maps is 1.5 km north and was not used.',
  [L('Parks Canada: Davis','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/visit/posteeclusage-lockstation/ecluse-lock-davis'),
   L('rideau-info.com: Davis lock','https://rideau-info.com/canal/locks/38-davis.html'),RCH],brick='no'),
N('narrows-lock-blockhouse','Narrows Lock blockhouse','Rideau Lakes','on','fort','standing',44.70262,-76.29535,'exact','public','drive','canal,forts','1832–1833',
  "The Narrows lock was cut where Big Rideau and Upper Rideau lakes met in a channel only about 30 m wide; it is the first of 31 locks on the descent to the Ottawa River and, Parks Canada says, the busiest on the Rideau. The blockhouse was built in 1832–33 to Colonel By's pattern, one of only four completed on the canal. The federal heritage record calls it one of the few surviving blockhouses in Canada with a stone lower storey.",
  'A two-storey, pyramid-roofed blockhouse: a thick stone lower storey with an overhanging upper storey of squared timber, loopholes, and a second-floor door reached by an outside stair. Interior not checked.',
  'One of four blockhouses built to guard the Rideau, standing where two lakes meet.',
  RCHOURS+' The lockstation is at 1275 Narrows Lock Road, Rideau Lakes; Parks Canada lists parking. OpenStreetMap maps a Rideau Trail parking lot about 1.4 km northwest.',
  [L('Parks Canada: Narrows','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/rideau/visit/posteeclusage-lockstation/ecluse-lock-narrows'),
   L('Federal heritage record: Blockhouse, Narrows','https://www.pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_fhbro_eng.aspx?id=3897'),RCH],
  brick='no'),
N('old-stone-mill-delta','Old Stone Mill','Delta','on','industry','standing',44.61016,-76.12251,'exact','public','drive','','1810–',
  "Abel Stevens, a Loyalist from Vermont, built the first mill here about 1796. William Jones (with Ira Schofield, in the Delta walking tour) put up the present three-and-a-half-storey stone grist mill by 1810, laid out for Oliver Evans's automatic milling system. Parks Canada calls it the earliest surviving stone mill in Ontario; the walking tour says it stood in an artificial channel and acted as its own dam. A turbine shed was added in the 1860s. It is a national historic site, designated in 1970.",
  'The stone mill on Delta Creek with its 1860s turbine shed. Inside, the Delta Mill Society shows an operating waterwheel, millstones, a bolter, grain elevators and milling artifacts, and in summer grinds heritage wheat on 200-year-old millstones. Self-guided or free guided tours.',
  'The oldest stone mill in Ontario still grinding flour each summer.',
  'Delta Mill Society, near 44 Highway 42 (46 King Street), Delta. Open daily from the Victoria Day weekend to the Labour Day weekend; admission free, donations welcome (society website). Parking on Water Street just east of the mill.',
  [L('Parks Canada: Old Stone Mill National Historic Site','https://pc.gc.ca/apps/dfhd/page_nhs_eng.aspx?id=366'),
   L('Delta Mill Society','https://www.deltamill.org/'),L('Delta Mill Society: walking tour of Delta','https://www.deltamill.org/deltatour.html')],
  th=[44.60986,-76.12112],thn='Water Street parking, Delta',brick='no'),
N('st-pauls-church-delta','St. Paul\'s Church, Delta','Delta','on','worship','standing',44.60759,-76.12132,'site','view','drive','','1811–',
  "Baptists built this fieldstone church in 1811 and used it with the interior unfinished. Anglicans bought it in 1827 and shared it with the Baptists, and Methodists met here from 1843 to 1862; the Baptists left in 1864. It was renovated in 1868 and 1907, when new pews and windows went in, and restored in 1979 with help from the Ontario Heritage Foundation, which holds an easement on it. The Delta walking tour (2019) says it no longer serves as an Anglican church and that a committee of residents keeps it for weddings, recitals and reunions; the Register entry still calls it St. Paul's Church (Anglican).",
  'A Gothic Revival church of fieldstone stuccoed and scored to look like cut stone, with a crenellated square tower with corner pinnacles and a round date stone reading "St. Paul\'s erected 1811 A.D.", pointed stained-glass windows, and a bell of 1829 given by Chief Justice John Beverley Robinson. Inside, a patterned wood-slat ceiling, and the tower shows its timber framing and thick fieldstone walls. When it is open is not checked.',
  'An 1811 church shared by three denominations, with a bell that, the local story goes, was stolen and recovered cracked.',
  'At 77 King Street (Highway 42), Delta, on its own lawn. Seen from the road; the inside only at events (walking tour). Owner not checked.',
  [L('HistoricPlaces.ca: St. Paul\'s Church (Delta)','https://historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=19807'),
   L('Delta Mill Society: walking tour of Delta','https://www.deltamill.org/deltatour.html')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('lyndhurst-bridge','Lyndhurst Bridge','Lyndhurst','on','landmark','standing',44.54969,-76.12547,'exact','view','drive','','1857–',
  'A three-span stone arch bridge over the Gananoque River, of local sandstone, finished in 1857 and reportedly the oldest surviving bridge in Ontario. It was designed by John Donald Roddick, a local mill owner, and built by Miles Fulford and Simon Ransom. In 1986 a reinforced concrete frame was put inside the deck to carry the load.',
  'Three sandstone arches carrying Lyndhurst Road in a single lane.',
  'Possibly the oldest bridge in Ontario, still carrying traffic.',
  'A public road bridge in Lyndhurst, Leeds and the Thousand Islands. Look at it from the riverbank; a small lot on Water Street is about 100 m north (OpenStreetMap). Who owns the bank is not checked.',
  [W('Lyndhurst Bridge')],th=[44.55065,-76.12581],thn='Water Street lot, Lyndhurst',brick='no'),
N('brockville-railway-tunnel','Brockville Railway Tunnel','Brockville','on','rail','standing',44.59031,-75.68261,'site','public','walk','','1854–1860',
  "Canada's first railway tunnel, dug for the Brockville and Ottawa Railway to link Ottawa Valley timber with the St. Lawrence. Work began in September 1854 and the first train passed through on December 31, 1860. It runs under downtown, beneath the city hall (Victoria Hall, 1863–64), from Water Street north to Pearl Street. Canadian Pacific trains used it into the mid-1970s; the rails were removed in the 1980s, the city took it over in 1982, and it opened to the public in 2017.",
  'A lit walk of about half a kilometre through the tunnel on a gentle 1% slope, accessible for wheelchairs and strollers; Brockville Tourism suggests entering from the south portal on Water Street.',
  "Walk through Canada's first railway tunnel, under city hall.",
  "City of Brockville. Brockville Tourism: open daily 9 am to 9 pm in spring and summer, statutory holidays included, free (donations welcome); it was closed for the season when checked in November. Municipal lots near the south entrance (Blockhouse Island and Water Street) and a free gravel lot at the north entrance.",
  [W('Brockville Tunnel'),L('Brockville Tourism: Railway Tunnel','https://brockvilletourism.com/things-to-do/brockville-railway-tunnel/'),
   L('Brockville Daily: tunnel opens for 2026 season','https://www.brockvilledaily.ca/news/top-3-stories-in-brockville/brockville-railway-tunnel-opens-for-2026-season-with-free-daily-access-and-upgrades-nearly-complete/')],
  th=[44.59021,-75.68224],thn='Railway Tunnel lot',brick='?'),
N('murney-tower','Murney Tower','Kingston','on','fort','standing',44.22228,-76.49041,'exact','public','walk','forts','1846–',
  "A Martello tower built in 1846 during the Oregon Crisis, one of five works that guarded Kingston harbour, the naval dockyard and the Rideau Canal entrance. Work began on February 7 and ended on November 10, 1846; the Oregon treaty had already been settled in June. It was meant to be Murray Tower after the Master General of Ordnance, but the land was Murney Point, and an N fixed over the first R left the door stone reading \"MURNAY TOWER\". The Kingston Historical Society opened it as a museum in 1925.",
  'A round limestone tower in a dry ditch, with brickwork on the barracks and basement levels inside, two carronades in the barracks, caponiers with loopholes covering the ditch, and a 32-pounder Blomefield gun on an iron traverse track on the gun platform under a snow roof added two years after it was built. Wikipedia says it is open as a museum in summer, May through Labour Day.',
  'The only Kingston Martello tower you can go inside, still misspelled over the door.',
  'Murney Tower Museum, Kingston Historical Society, on King Street West by the lake. Hours not checked (the society\'s page could not be read); street parking only, not checked.',
  [W('Murney Tower'),L('Visit Kingston: Murney Tower','https://www.visitkingston.ca/listings/murney-tower-national-historic-site/')],brick='?'),
N('shoal-tower','Shoal Tower','Kingston','on','fort','standing',44.22883,-76.47814,'exact','view','walk','forts','1847–',
  'Built in 1847 as Victoria Tower on a shoal in the harbour, opposite city hall, one of four Martello towers raised in the 1840s to guard Kingston harbour and the entrance to the Rideau Canal. Its guns covered the approaches to the harbour. It was later abandoned, and was named a national historic site in 1930; it is part of the Kingston Fortifications.',
  'A limestone tower about 11 m high and 16.5 m across standing in the water of Confederation Basin. Not open to the public (Wikipedia: only Murney Tower is).',
  'A stone gun tower standing in the harbour, across from city hall.',
  'Seen from Confederation Park and the waterfront in front of Kingston City Hall. Not open.',
  [W('Shoal Tower')],brick='no'),
N('cathcart-tower','Cathcart Tower','Kingston','on','fort','standing',44.22542,-76.45413,'exact','view','boat','forts','1848–',
  "Built in 1848 on Cedar Island to cover the eastern approaches to Kingston harbour, one of four Martello towers of the 1840s. Alexander Mackenzie, later Canada's second prime minister, was a foreman on the job; 17 men of his crew drowned when their boat capsized coming back from the island, and Hamilton Cove became Deadman's Bay. When war was averted the tower was used for a time as a barracks for Fort Henry's soldiers, then abandoned.",
  'A limestone tower about 11 m high and 16.5 m across inside a shallow ditch and a glacis that runs to the shore on three sides. Not open (Wikipedia: only Murney Tower is).',
  'The tower whose building crew drowned in Deadman\'s Bay.',
  'On Cedar Island, within Thousand Islands National Park (Parks Canada). Seen across the water from the Fort Henry shore; by boat otherwise. Rules for landing on the island not checked.',
  [W('Cathcart Tower')],brick='no'),
N('fort-frontenac-bastion','Fort Frontenac: Bastion St. Michel','Kingston','on','fort','traces',44.23395,-76.47882,'area','view','walk','forts','1673–',
  "A French trading post built in July 1673 at the mouth of the Cataraqui River, later named for Governor Frontenac. La Salle rebuilt it in stone bastions and walls from 1675. It was razed in 1689, rebuilt in 1695, and destroyed by the British in 1758; the British rebuilt it from 1783, and as Tête-de-Pont Barracks and then Fort Frontenac it has been a military post ever since. In 1984 the city redesigned the corner of Ontario and Place d'Armes streets so the northwest bastion (Bastion St. Michel) and a curtain wall could be excavated and partly rebuilt; intact remains of the east bastion were found in 2020.",
  "The excavated and partly reconstructed footings of the northwest bastion and curtain wall at Ontario and Place d'Armes streets, beside the military buildings still called Fort Frontenac. The pin is on Ontario Street at the northwest corner of the fort grounds, read off the aerial; the bastion itself was not made out, so the pin is only near it.",
  "Footings of the French fort from which Kingston grew, laid out at a street corner.",
  "Ontario Street at Place d'Armes, downtown Kingston. The fort grounds are an active Canadian Army post (the Canadian Army Command and Staff College); the bastion is seen from the street. Municipal parking on Brock Street.",
  [W('Fort Frontenac')],brick='?'),
N('fort-henry-kingston','Fort Henry','Kingston','on','fort','standing',44.23088,-76.45902,'site','public','drive','forts','1832–',
  "A fortification went up on Point Henry in the War of 1812 to protect the naval dockyard on Point Frederick. A much larger stone fort replaced it in the 1830s, and the casemated stores and magazines of the advanced battery facing the lake were finished in 1843. It was restored as a Great Depression work project and opened as a living museum, with the Fort Henry Guard, on August 1, 1938. Wikipedia files it among First World War internment camps and Second World War prisoner-of-war camps.",
  'The restored stone fort with its casemates and the advanced battery, where the Fort Henry Guard give demonstrations and tours; self-guided tours too, and evening tours of its \"haunted\" past by a local company.',
  'A restored British fort where the guard still drills.',
  'Fort Henry National Historic Site, run by the St. Lawrence Parks Commission, on Fort Henry Drive. Seasonal; the fort\'s website said the season had ended when checked in November, and hours and admission were not checked. Parking on Fort Henry Drive.',
  [W('Fort Henry National Historic Site'),L('Fort Henry','https://www.forthenry.com/')],
  th=[44.23203,-76.45878],thn='Fort Henry parking lot',brick='?'),
N('kingston-penitentiary','Kingston Penitentiary','Kingston','on','inst','standing',44.21935,-76.51347,'site','permit','walk','','1833–2013',
  "Built in 1833–34 and opened on June 1, 1835 as the Provincial Penitentiary of Upper Canada, on a site chosen for its health, its water access and its \"abundant quantities of fine limestone\". A dome joining four cellblocks was added in 1859–61. The cells first measured 73.7 cm wide by 244 cm deep. Riots in 1971 and 1975 led to an inquiry into the penitentiary system. When it closed on September 30, 2013 it was one of the oldest prisons in continuous use in the world.",
  'The walled prison on King Street West, its grey limestone gatehouse with a columned front and a domed cupola, and inside the dome and cellblocks, seen on guided tours only. Across the road is Cedarhedge, the warden\'s residence of 1870–73, now the Canada\'s Penitentiary Museum, and to the north the closed Prison for Women (1934–2000).',
  'One of the oldest prisons in continuous use anywhere when it closed in 2013.',
  'Kingston Pen Tours, 560 King Street West; the tour site\'s admissions page said \"Currently Closed for the Season\" in a search result and could not be opened. Tour dates and prices not checked. Inside only on a ticketed tour.',
  [W('Kingston Penitentiary'),L('Kingston Pen Tours: admissions','https://kingstonpentour.getanchor.io/visit/admissions/index.html')],
  th=[44.21996,-76.51220],thn='Lot east of the penitentiary',brick='no'),
N('rockwood-asylum','Rockwood Asylum','Kingston','on','inst','standing',44.21562,-76.52275,'site','view','walk','','1859–2000',
  "One of the first criminal asylums in Upper Canada, set up in 1859 on John Cartwright's 40-acre waterfront estate just west of Portsmouth, to relieve overcrowding in the local jails and Kingston Penitentiary. William Coverdale designed the building; John A. Macdonald inspected the site during construction. It remained a psychiatric hospital in one form or another until it closed in 2000.",
  "Coverdale's four-storey Italian-style stone building, vacant since 2000 (Wikipedia), a few metres inland from the lake. Seen from outside only.",
  'A Victorian asylum for the criminally insane, empty since 2000.',
  'Vacant building on its grounds off King Street West (OpenStreetMap address: Penrose Lane). Who owns the grounds now and whether they are open is not checked: view from public streets only.',
  [W('Rockwood Asylum')],brick='no'),
N('bellevue-house','Bellevue House','Kingston','on','landmark','standing',44.22303,-76.50411,'exact','public','walk','','1840–',
  'An Italian villa built about 1840 for Charles Hales, a Kingston merchant, and one of the first of its style in Canada: an L-shaped plan with two wings off a square central tower, windows and gables of different shapes, and several small balconies. John A. Macdonald, later the first prime minister, lived here with his wife Isabella and their son in 1848–49. It opened as a national historic park in 1967.',
  'White stucco walls with green trim and balconies over a stone base. Restored inside and out to the late 1840s, in its original colours and wallpaper patterns, and furnished with pieces of the 1820s to 1840s; guided and self-guided tours with interpreters in period dress.',
  "The odd-shaped villa where Canada's first prime minister lived for a year.",
  'Bellevue House National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 35 Centre Street. 2026: open Thursday to Monday from May 16 to June 30 and from September 8 to October 14, daily July 1 to September 7, and closed from October 15 to May 15, 2027. Fees not checked. Street parking nearby, not checked.',
  [W('Bellevue House'),L('Parks Canada: Bellevue House hours','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/on/bellevue/visit/heures-hours')],brick='no'),
N('kingston-city-hall','Kingston City Hall and Market Square','Kingston','on','civic','standing',44.22987,-76.48043,'exact','view','walk','','1843–1844',
  "Built in 1843–44 after a fire in April 1840 burned the market and much of downtown, when Kingston was the capital of the Province of Canada; George Browne was the architect. The limestone building was T-shaped, with a market wing (the \"shambles\") running back toward King Street. The wing burned in 1865 and was rebuilt smaller, and a fire in 1908 destroyed the main dome, which was rebuilt. Market Square behind it dates from the 1784 town plan and holds the oldest public market in Ontario.",
  'The domed limestone city hall facing the lake, with its portico (removed in 1956 and restored in 1966) and, in Memorial Hall in the north wing, twelve stained-glass windows for First World War battles. The market runs on Tuesdays, Thursdays and Saturdays from April to November, with antiques on Sundays. When the public can go in is not checked.',
  'The city hall built for a capital that moved away.',
  'City of Kingston, on Springer Market Square. Seen from the square and Confederation Park. Municipal lots nearby; interior hours not checked.',
  [W('Kingston City Hall (Ontario)')],brick='no'),
N('st-georges-cathedral-kingston','St. George\'s Cathedral','Kingston','on','worship','standing',44.22945,-76.48305,'exact','view','walk','','1825–',
  "The parish began in a wooden church of 1792 across from Market Square, the first church in the Kingston area. The present church went up at King and Johnson streets in 1825–28. William Coverdale enlarged it in 1838–40 with a rebuilt steeple, and the Doric portico followed in 1842. It became the cathedral of the Anglican Diocese of Ontario in 1862. Transepts, choir and dome were added in 1891–94, and a fire in 1899 badly damaged the interior, which S. George Curry and Joseph Power restored in 1899–1900.",
  'The domed cathedral with its Doric portico. Inside, stained-glass windows of soldier saints in the Royal Military College gallery. When it is open to visitors is not checked.',
  'A cathedral that has been enlarged, burned and rebuilt since 1825.',
  'Anglican Diocese of Ontario, King Street at Johnson Street. Seen from the street; visiting hours not checked.',
  [W("St. George's Cathedral (Kingston, Ontario)")],brick='no'),
N('frontenac-county-court-house','Frontenac County Court House','Kingston','on','civic','standing',44.22781,-76.48968,'exact','view','walk','','1858–',
  'A Neoclassical limestone court house designed by Edward Horsey and built by Scobell and Tossell; it opened in 1858. After a fire in 1874 John Power added the domed tower, and a three-tier fountain was put in front in 1903. It was named a national historic site in 1980.',
  "The domed limestone court house with the royal coat of arms on its front, looking south over City Park to the lake.",
  'A domed court house on the edge of the park.',
  'Frontenac County court house, 21 Court Street (OpenStreetMap address), between Barrie and West streets. Seen from City Park. Interior and hours not checked.',
  [W('Frontenac County Court House')],brick='no'),
]
